# Append-only log: union-merge keeps BOTH sides instead of producing a conflict.
# "union" is a built-in git merge driver, so no local config is needed.
#
# READ THIS BEFORE TRUSTING IT. Git honours merge drivers LOCALLY ONLY. GitHub
# does not run them server-side: it computes mergeability with a plain
# three-way merge and reports CONFLICTING for any PR whose PROGRESS.md region
# also moved on main. A PR can therefore merge perfectly cleanly in a worktree
# and still be blocked in the UI.
#
# An earlier version of this comment claimed this attribute "eliminates the
# recurring PROGRESS.md merge conflicts across concurrent Claude sessions
# (which were blocking ~6 of 9 open PRs)". It does not. It hides them locally,
# which is worse than not having it, because the branch looks healthy right up
# until GitHub refuses to merge it.
#
# What this attribute actually buys: local merges of PROGRESS.md resolve
# without hand-editing. That is real and worth keeping.
#
# What it cannot buy: a green Merge button. To clear a CONFLICTING PR you must
# merge main INTO the branch locally (the driver resolves PROGRESS.md) and then
# push, which makes main's tip an ancestor and leaves GitHub nothing to merge.
# On this repo that push is expensive: branch protection sets
# dismiss_stale_reviews AND require_last_push_approval, so it permanently
# destroys any approval the PR already carried. See MERGE_WORKFLOW.md, section
# "PROGRESS.md conflicts, and why GitHub still says CONFLICTING".
PROGRESS.md merge=union

# Git hooks are executed by /bin/sh. With core.autocrlf=true (the default on
# Windows here) a checkout would rewrite these to CRLF, and a CRLF shebang
# fails with "bad interpreter". Pin them to LF so the hook survives a clone.
.githooks/** text eol=lf
